\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A modular cancellation and a surviving component}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Take one vertex, no edges and site weight #{1}. Let
#{G=GL_2(\mathbb F_2)} act on #{F=\mathbb F_2^2}. In a basis
#{e_1,e_2}, the orbit of #{e_1} consists of #{e_1,e_2,e_1+e_2}:
each nonzero vector can be extended to a basis. Thus no nonzero vector
is fixed by all of #{G}. The partition coefficient is #{3=1} in the
field and the three vectors sum to zero. There are #{3} choices for
the first basis vector and #{2} for its complement, so #{|G|=6=0} in
the field. The normalized mean exists although the normalized group
projection in \ref{fdip-00WP} cannot be formed.}

\p{On the same graph over the reals, let the sign group act on the
Euclidean plane by #{(a,b)\mapsto(a,-b)} for the nonidentity element.
The orbit of #{(1,1)} has two points. Its uniform mean is #{(1,0)},
which lies on the fixed axis.}
